      "locked": false,
      "text": "Theory of Computation:\n\n- Computation is nothing but the processing of doing some work/calculation.\n- Consider everything a problem. A problem can be thought of as a function. We define what the output is given an input.\n- A problem is solvable if there exists an algorithm which gives an output for every input.\n- Now, if there is an algorithm for a problem, we make a program out of it and Execute the program.\n- The problems which can be computed/executed are the solvable problems. Problems which don't have any algorithm cannot be computed and hence can't be solved.\n\n- Theory of Computation, talks about whether a problem is solvable/computable or not.\n- If a problem is computable/solvable, we are also interested in WHAT KIND OF problem it is. i.e. we dig more into properties like\n  how much time/space it takes and classify problems based on the properties.\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Theory of Computation:\n\n- Computation is nothing but the processing of doing some work/calculation.\n- Consider everything a problem. A problem can be thought of as a function. We define what the output is given an input.\n- A problem is solvable if there exists an algorithm which gives an output for every input.\n- Now, if there is an algorithm for a problem, we make a program out of it and Execute the program.\n- The problems which can be computed/executed are the solvable problems. Problems which don't have any algorithm cannot be computed and hence can't be solved.\n\n- Theory of Computation, talks about whether a problem is solvable/computable or not.\n- If a problem is computable/solvable, we are also interested in WHAT KIND OF problem it is. i.e. we dig more into properties like\n  how much time/space it takes and classify problems based on the properties.\n\n",
